# Defect 2522's row 2, a known cost by the author's ruling B of 2026-11-20,
# lane-signals-2: a correct program whose handler jumps out of itself by a
# jump no function of the C library makes (the inline `__builtin_setjmp`
# pair on x86-64, the same jump written by hand on arm64:
# `fixedbugs-2522-an-inline-jump-out-of-a-handler.h`), and which, the signal
# over, calls the function from far deeper.
# Why it is refused: the runtime sees the handler begin and sees no event for
# this jump, so the handler is never seen to end, and the runtime cannot tell
# this program from one that jumps within its handler and then calls the
# function inside the signal, where neither its memory nor the C library's is
# safe. Running the function here would be a bet on memory; robustness ranks
# first (CLAUDE.md § Precedence), so the refusal stands.
# The ruling: every refusal resting on a handler not seen to end takes words
# never false of a correct program. Until then this one said *C called it
# while handling a signal*, false here (20 runs of 20 at `-O0` in the
# emulated Linux x86-64 image, lane-signals, 2026-11-20).
extern "fixedbugs-2522-an-inline-jump-out-of-a-handler.h"
    function inline_install(sig: i32) -> i32
    function inline_escape(sig: i32) -> i32
    function inline_call(cb: (function(i64) -> i64)) -> i64

extern "signal.h"
    constant SIGUSR1: i32

function answer(n: i64) -> i64
    return n + 1

function main()
    print("installed ", inline_install(sig: SIGUSR1))
    print("escaped ", inline_escape(sig: SIGUSR1))
    print("called ", inline_call(cb: answer))
